\details{
\strong{Vertical datum.} \code{z = 0} is the ground \strong{at the axis} (the DTM elevation
at the station), not the ground under each point. Normalising point by point
would flatten the road crown, which is exactly what \code{ajustement} fits. Height
above the local ground is available per point as \code{hauteur_sol}.

\strong{The five edge families.} They are defined here, not by any engine
(\code{dsr_measure()} gives the drivable width, \code{dsr_emprise_certu()} a normative
footprint; neither yields the five). All are derived from \emph{this} profile:
\itemize{
\item \code{road} -- the \strong{platform} (road prism): the run around the axis where the
ground stays within \code{tol_plateforme} of the plane fitted on the central
window. It stops at the ditch, the cut slope or the fill toe.
\item \code{drivable} -- the \strong{carriageway}: the run where the ground stays within
\code{tol_chaussee} of the fitted crown parabola, \strong{clipped to \code{road}}. Clipping
is what makes \code{drivable <= road} a property rather than a hope.
\item \code{shoulder} -- what is left of the platform on each side, hence \strong{two rows}
(\code{cote} = \code{"gauche"} / \code{"droite"}), possibly of zero width.
\item \code{rescue} -- the width usable by an \strong{emergency vehicle}: grown outwards
from \code{road} while the ground stays crossable (cross-slope under
\code{pente_max}), then clipped to \code{right_of_way}. Neither the ditch, nor the
cut slope, nor the undergrowth.
\item \code{right_of_way} -- the cleared corridor: the run around the axis free of any
echo in the \strong{trunk band} (\code{h_obstacle} m above the ground), \strong{unioned with
\code{road}}.
}

The four symmetric families are therefore \strong{nested by construction}:
\verb{drivable <= road <= rescue <= right_of_way}. It is a property of the code,
not a hope about the terrain.

\strong{Trunks, not canopy.} The corridor is read on the trunk band and not on the
canopy, and that is a measurement, not a preference: on the \code{dessertR} sample
tile (Lozère, closed high forest) the crowns close \strong{over} a 3.6 m track, so
a \emph{"no echo above 2 m"} rule returns a zero-width gap and a right of way equal
to the platform -- a family that no longer says anything. Trunks stand back:
23 m of clear corridor at the very same station.

\strong{Cost.} Only a rectangle of \code{epaisseur_m} x 2 \code{demi_largeur} is read, and
only from the tiles whose LAS header meets it. A click reads a few thousand
points, not a tile.

\strong{Degraded modes all return \code{NULL}} -- never an error, never a half-filled
list: no segment within \code{tolerance_m}, no \code{rlas} (the point-cloud reader, a
\code{dessertR} dependency), no tile under the station, or an empty slice. The
\code{cli} message says which one.
